=== lesson
id: py-sets
title: Sets
summary: A set is a collection that holds each item only once and has no order. Sets remove duplicates, answer "have I seen this before?", and combine with other sets to find what two groups share or don't share.

=== learn
### A set keeps each item once
A **set** is a collection in which every item appears **at most once**. You write a set with curly brackets, like a dictionary, but with single items instead of key: value pairs:

```python
nums = {3, 1, 3, 2, 1}
print(nums)
print(len(nums))
```

```output
{1, 2, 3}
3
```

You typed five numbers, but the set kept only three. The second 3 and the second 1 were dropped, because the set already had them. Duplicates are never an error: they quietly disappear.

Notice also that Python printed the items in an order of its own. Step 5 explains why you can't rely on that order.

Sets are the right tool for questions like "which different values are there?" and "have I seen this before?".

### set() and the {} trap
`set(...)` builds a set from a list, or from anything else you can loop over, and drops the duplicates:

```python
rolls = [6, 2, 6, 6, 1]
print(set(rolls))
print(len(set(rolls)))
```

```output
{1, 2, 6}
3
```

To make an **empty** set, write `set()`. You can't use `{}` for that: dictionaries had curly brackets first, so `{}` means an empty **dictionary**.

```python
print(type({}))
print(type(set()))
print(set())
```

```output
<class 'dict'>
<class 'set'>
set()
```

Python even prints an empty set as `set()`, so it can't be mistaken for `{}`.

| You write | You get |
| --- | --- |
| `{1, 2}` | a set with two items |
| `{"a": 1}` | a dictionary, because the colon makes a pair |
| `{}` | an empty dictionary |
| `set()` | an empty set |

### Adding and checking: add() and in
`s.add(x)` puts `x` into the set. Adding an item that is already there changes nothing: no error, and no second copy. `x in s` checks whether `x` is in the set, and `x not in s` checks that it isn't.

```python
seen = set()
seen.add(5)
seen.add(8)
seen.add(5)
print(len(seen))
print(5 in seen)
print(7 in seen)
```

```output
2
True
False
```

The third `add` did nothing, because 5 was already in the set.

A set finds an item the same way a dictionary finds a key, using its hash, so `in` stays quick even when the set holds a million items. A list has to check its items one by one.

> Sets use `.add()`, not `.append()`. "Append" means "put at the end", and a set has no end. `seen.append(5)` stops with `AttributeError: 'set' object has no attribute 'append'`. An **AttributeError** means the value has no method with that name.

### Removing: remove() or discard()
Two methods take an item out of a set. They only behave differently when the item is **missing**:

| Method | Item is in the set | Item is missing |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `s.remove(x)` | removes it | stops with a KeyError |
| `s.discard(x)` | removes it | does nothing |

```python
nums = {1, 2, 3}
nums.discard(2)
nums.discard(9)
print(nums)
nums.remove(9)
```

```output
{1, 3}
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 5, in <module>
    nums.remove(9)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~^^^
KeyError: 9
```

`discard(2)` removed the 2. `discard(9)` found no 9 and quietly did nothing. `remove(9)` found no 9 either, and stopped the program.

Use `remove` when a missing item would mean a mistake you want to hear about. Use `discard` when "it might not be there" is fine.

### No order, no indexes
A set has no first item and no second item: no positions at all. So square brackets don't work on a set:

```python
nums = {10, 3, 7}
print(nums)
print(nums[0])
```

```output
{10, 3, 7}
Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "main.py", line 3, in <module>
    print(nums[0])
          ~~~~^^^
TypeError: 'set' object is not subscriptable
```

"Not subscriptable" means "can't be used with square brackets".

When Python prints a set, it shows the items in whatever order it happens to store them. Small whole numbers often come out looking sorted, but `{10, 3, 7}` didn't. With strings, real Python can even print a different order each time you run the same program. So never rely on the order of a set.

Because order doesn't matter, two sets are equal (`==`) when they hold the same items: `{1, 2} == {2, 1}` is `True`.

### sorted(): the items as an ordered list
When you need the items in order, use **`sorted()`**. It takes the items, puts them in order (numbers from smallest to largest, strings in alphabetical order) and gives you back a new **list**:

```python
fruits = {"pear", "fig", "apple"}
print(sorted(fruits))
print(sorted({10, 3, 7}))
```

```output
['apple', 'fig', 'pear']
[3, 7, 10]
```

The set itself doesn't change. `sorted()` works on lists too. Unlike `.sort()` from the lists lesson, it leaves the original alone and returns a sorted copy.

You can loop over a set with `for x in fruits:`, but the items come in an unknown order. Loop over `sorted(fruits)` when the order matters.

> This course always prints `sorted(...)` of a set of strings, never the set itself, so that the output is the same every time.

### Removing duplicates from a list
Turning a list into a set is the quickest way to drop its duplicates:

```python
names = ["Bo", "Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Ada"]
print(len(set(names)))
print(sorted(set(names)))
```

```output
3
['Ada', 'Bo', 'Cy']
```

`len(set(names))` counts the **different** names, and `sorted(set(names))` lists them in alphabetical order.

A set forgets the original order, though. To keep each name's first appearance in its original place, loop with a **seen set**:

```python
unique = []
seen = set()
for name in names:
    if name not in seen:
        seen.add(name)
        unique.append(name)
print(unique)
```

```output
['Bo', 'Ada', 'Cy']
```

A name goes into `unique` only the first time it comes up. After that it's in `seen`, so the `if` skips it. Animation 1 shows every step.

### Union and intersection
Python combines two sets with operators. Each operator builds a **new** set and leaves both originals unchanged.

```python
a = {1, 2, 3, 4}
b = {3, 4, 5}
print(a | b)
print(a & b)
```

```output
{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
{3, 4}
```

- `a | b` is the **union**: every item that is in `a`, in `b`, or in both. The `|` symbol is called a **pipe**. Read `a | b` as "in a *or* in b". 3 and 4 are in both sets, but a set holds them only once.
- `a & b` is the **intersection**: only the items that are in `a` **and** in `b`. Read `&` as "and".

Swapping the two sets doesn't change these results: `b | a` is the same set as `a | b`, and `b & a` is the same as `a & b`.

### Difference: what one set has that the other doesn't
`a - b` is the **difference**: the items of `a` that are **not** in `b`.

```python
a = {1, 2, 3, 4}
b = {3, 4, 5}
print(a - b)
print(b - a)
```

```output
{1, 2}
{5}
```

Here the order **does** matter. `a - b` starts from `a` and takes away everything that is also in `b`. `b - a` starts from `b` and takes away everything that is also in `a`.

A real use, with names:

```python
invited = {"Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Di"}
replied = {"Bo", "Di"}
print(sorted(invited - replied))
```

```output
['Ada', 'Cy']
```

Ada and Cy were invited but haven't replied, so they need a reminder.

### List, set or dictionary?
|  | list | set | dictionary |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Written as | `[3, 1, 3]` | `{3, 1}` | `{"a": 3}` |
| Duplicates | allowed | dropped | each key once |
| Order | kept, with indexes | none | keys keep the order they were added |
| Look up by | position: `nums[0]` | only `in` | key: `d["a"]` |
| Good for | items in a sequence | unique items, "seen it?" | finding a value by its key |

One more rule that sets share with dictionary keys: set items must be **immutable**. Strings, numbers and tuples are fine, but a list can't go in a set: `{[1, 2]}` stops with `TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'`.

=== keypoints
- A set holds each item at most once: `{3, 1, 3}` has two items. `set(a_list)` drops a list's duplicates.
- `set()` makes an empty set. `{}` makes an empty **dictionary**.
- `s.add(x)` adds an item (nothing happens if it's already there). `x in s` checks whether it's there.
- `s.remove(x)` raises a `KeyError` if `x` is missing; `s.discard(x)` does nothing instead.
- Sets have no order and no indexes, so `s[0]` is a `TypeError`. `sorted(s)` gives the items as a list in order.
- `a | b` (union): in either set. `a & b` (intersection): in both. `a - b` (difference): in `a` but not in `b`.
- Set items must be immutable, like dictionary keys.

=== example: Two class lists
intro: Set operators answer questions about two groups of people. Every set of names is printed with `sorted()`.
```python
monday = {"Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Di"}
tuesday = {"Cy", "Di", "Ed"}
print(len(monday), "came on Monday")
print("Both days:", sorted(monday & tuesday))
print("Either day:", sorted(monday | tuesday))
print("Only Monday:", sorted(monday - tuesday))
print("Only Tuesday:", sorted(tuesday - monday))
print("Ed" in monday)
```
1-2: Two sets of names: who came to class on Monday, and who came on Tuesday.
3: `len()` counts the items in a set, the same as for a list: 4 people.
4: `&` keeps the people in both sets: Cy and Di. `sorted()` turns the result into a list in alphabetical order.
5: `|` keeps everyone who is in either set. Cy and Di are in both, but they appear only once.
6: `monday - tuesday` starts with Monday's people and takes away anyone who also came on Tuesday: Ada and Bo.
7: The other way round: Tuesday's people minus Monday's. Only Ed is left.
8: `in` checks one item. Ed is not in the Monday set, so this prints False.

=== example: Tidying a list of tags
intro: Remove duplicates with `set()`, change the set with `add` and `discard`, and print it in order with `sorted()`.
```python
tags = ["python", "code", "fun", "code", "python"]
unique = set(tags)
print(len(tags), "tags,", len(unique), "different")
unique.add("loops")
unique.add("fun")
unique.discard("cats")
print(sorted(unique))
for tag in sorted(unique):
    print("#" + tag)
```
1: A list with duplicates: "python" and "code" appear twice each.
2: `set(tags)` keeps one copy of each tag: python, code and fun.
3: The list still has 5 items, but there are only 3 different tags.
4: "loops" is new, so the set grows to 4 items.
5: "fun" is already in the set, so this does nothing: no error, and no second copy.
6: "cats" isn't in the set. `discard` doesn't mind; `remove` would have raised a KeyError.
7: `sorted()` gives the tags as a list in alphabetical order.
8-9: Looping over `sorted(unique)` visits the tags in that same order, every time.

=== animation: Removing duplicates with a seen set
view: set seen
array unique
vars name
intro: Watch the seen set remember every name, while unique keeps each name once, in its original order. The bubbles in a set have no order.
```python
names = ["Bo", "Ada", "Bo", "Cy", "Ada"]
seen = set()
unique = []
for name in names:
    if name not in seen:
        seen.add(name)
        unique.append(name)
print(unique)
```
1: Five names, but only three different ones.
2: seen starts as an empty set. It will remember every name the loop has already met.
3: unique is the list being built: each name once, in the order it first appears.
4: The loop hands out the next name. After the last one, the loop ends.
5: name is {name!r}. Is it not in seen yet? {name not in seen}, so it is {'new: keep it' if name not in seen else 'a repeat: skip it'}.
6: Add '{name}' to seen, so any later copy of it gets skipped...
7: ...and append '{name}' to unique.
8: Done. unique is ['Bo', 'Ada', 'Cy']: every name once, in the order it first appeared.

=== animation: Intersection and difference by hand
view: set both
set only_a
vars x
intro: A loop that sorts every item of a into one of two new sets. It builds the same sets that a & b and a - b give.
```python
a = {1, 2, 3, 4}
b = {3, 4, 5}
both = set()
only_a = set()
for x in a:
    if x in b:
        both.add(x)
    else:
        only_a.add(x)
print(both, only_a)
print(a & b, a - b)
```
1: a holds 1, 2, 3 and 4.
2: b holds 3, 4 and 5.
3: both will collect the items that are in a AND in b.
4: only_a will collect the items of a that are NOT in b.
5: The loop takes the next item of a. A set has no positions, but a loop still visits every item exactly once.
6: x is {x}. Is {x} in b too? {x in b}.
7: Yes: {x} is in both sets, so it goes into both.
9: No: {x} is in a but not in b, so it goes into only_a.
10: Done. both is {both} and only_a is {only_a}.
11: The & and - operators build exactly the same two sets, in one step each.

=== task: predict easy
title: Duplicates vanish
code:
```python
nums = {4, 2, 4, 4, 2}
print(len(nums))
nums.add(2)
nums.add(7)
print(len(nums))
```
wrong[5\n7]: A set holds each item only once. The duplicate 4s and 2s were dropped as soon as the set was made, so it starts with 2 items, not 5. Then only 7 is new.
wrong[5\n6]: A set never keeps duplicates. `{4, 2, 4, 4, 2}` holds just 4 and 2, so its length is 2, and after adding 7 it is 3.
wrong[2\n4]: `nums.add(2)` doesn't add a second 2, because 2 is already in the set. Only `add(7)` adds something, so the length goes from 2 to 3.
hint: How many **different** numbers are inside the curly brackets?
hint: Adding an item that is already in a set changes nothing.
right: The set keeps one copy of each value, so `{4, 2, 4, 4, 2}` holds only 4 and 2: length `2`. Adding 2 again does nothing, and adding 7 makes it `3`.
wrong: A set drops duplicates. The five numbers you typed contain only two different values, 4 and 2, so the first length is `2`. `nums.add(2)` changes nothing because 2 is already there, and `nums.add(7)` adds a new item, so the second length is `3`.

=== task: mcq easy
title: An empty set
prompt: Which of these makes an **empty set**?
- `{}` :: This is the classic trap. Dictionaries had curly brackets first, so `{}` is an empty **dictionary**: `type({})` is `<class 'dict'>`.
* `set()` :: Right. `set()` with nothing inside the brackets makes an empty set, and Python prints it as `set()`.
- `[]` :: Square brackets make a list. `[]` is an empty list, which allows duplicates and has positions.
- `{0}` :: This is a set, but not an empty one: it holds one item, the number 0, so its length is 1.
hint: One of these is a dictionary, one is a list, and one isn't empty.
hint: Curly brackets with nothing inside already mean something else in Python.
right: An empty set has to be written `set()`, because `{}` was already taken by dictionaries. Python prints an empty set as `set()` for the same reason.
wrong: `{}` looks like it should be an empty set, but it is an empty dictionary. The only way to write an empty set is `set()`.

=== task: mcq easy
title: remove or discard?
prompt: What happens when this program runs?
code:
```python
pets = {"cat", "dog"}
pets.discard("fish")
pets.remove("fish")
print(len(pets))
```
- It prints `2` :: `discard` is happy to find no fish, but `remove` is not. The program stops before the `print` line runs.
- It stops with a KeyError on line 2 :: `discard` never raises an error. When the item is missing, it does nothing.
* It stops with a KeyError on line 3 :: Right. `remove` insists that the item is in the set. There is no "fish", so Python stops with `KeyError: 'fish'`.
- It prints `1` :: Neither line can take anything out, because "fish" was never in the set. And line 3 stops the program before anything is printed.
hint: Both methods are asked to take out "fish", which isn't in the set.
hint: One of the two methods does nothing when the item is missing; the other one raises an error.
right: `discard("fish")` finds no fish and does nothing. `remove("fish")` finds no fish and raises `KeyError: 'fish'`, so the program stops on line 3 and never reaches `print`.
wrong: "fish" isn't in the set. `discard` does nothing in that case, so line 2 is fine. `remove` raises a KeyError in that case, so the program stops on line 3, before anything is printed.

=== task: fill medium
title: Friends in common
prompt: Ada and Alan want to know which friends they have in common. Fill in the blank so that the program prints exactly:

```output
['Cy', 'Di']
```
code:
```python
ada = {"Bo", "Cy", "Di"}
alan = {"Cy", "Di", "Ed"}
common = ada ?? alan
print(sorted(common))
```
answer: &
wrong[-]: `ada - alan` gives the friends Ada has that Alan doesn't: `['Bo']`. For the friends in both sets, use `&`.
wrong[+]: Sets can't be joined with `+`: this raises `TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'set' and 'set'`. The operator for "in both" is `&`.
wrong[and]: `and` is the True/False operator from the booleans lesson, not a set operator. With two sets it doesn't combine them; here it gives back the whole second set, `['Cy', 'Di', 'Ed']`. The set operator for "in both" is `&`.
hint: You want the people who are in Ada's set **and** in Alan's set.
hint: Which operator keeps only the items that are in both sets?
right: `ada & alan` is the intersection: only the friends who are in both sets, Cy and Di. `sorted()` turns them into a list in alphabetical order.
wrong: The friends in common are in Ada's set **and** in Alan's set, which is the intersection, `&`. The pipe `|` would give everyone in either set (Bo, Cy, Di, Ed), and `-` would give the friends only Ada has.

=== task: cells medium
title: How big is the set now?
prompt: What is `len(s)` right after each line runs? Watch the capital letters.
code:
```python
s = {"red"}
s.add("blue")
s.add("red")
s.discard("green")
s.remove("blue")
s.add("Red")
s.discard("red")
```
labels: line 1 | line 2 | line 3 | line 4 | line 5 | line 6 | line 7
answer: 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 1
wrong[1 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 1]: Line 3 adds "red", which is already in the set, so the length stays at 2. A set never holds two copies.
wrong[1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 1 | 0]: `"Red"` with a capital R is a different string from `"red"`. Line 6 adds a new item (length 2), and line 7 removes only the lowercase "red", leaving "Red" (length 1).
wrong[1 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 0]: `discard("green")` on line 4 does nothing, because "green" isn't in the set. So the length stays at 2 there.
hint: Adding something that is already in the set changes nothing, and so does discarding something that isn't there.
hint: `"Red"` and `"red"` are different strings, because capital letters count.
hint: Before line 6 the set is `{"red"}`. Line 6 adds a new item, and line 7 takes one away.
right: Only real changes count: "blue" is new (2), "red" is already there (still 2), there is no "green" to discard (still 2), "blue" is removed (1), "Red" with a capital R is a new item (2), and discarding "red" leaves only "Red" (1).
wrong: Go line by line and ask whether each line really changes the set. Line 3 adds a "red" that is already there, so nothing changes. Line 4 discards a "green" that isn't there, so nothing changes. Line 6 adds "Red", which is a different string from "red", so the set grows. Line 7 takes out the lowercase "red", leaving 1 item.

=== task: multi medium
title: Which ones are True?
prompt: Choose **every** expression that is `True`.
code:
```python
a = {1, 2, 3}
b = {2, 3, 4}
```
* `{1, 2} == {2, 1}` :: Right. Sets have no order, so two sets with the same items are equal, whichever order you write them in.
- `len(a | b) == 6` :: `a | b` is `{1, 2, 3, 4}`. 2 and 3 are in both sets, but the union holds them only once, so its length is 4.
* `4 in (a | b)` :: Right. The union holds everything from either set, and 4 is in `b`.
- `1 in (a & b)` :: The intersection keeps only items that are in both sets. 1 is in `a` but not in `b`, so `a & b` is `{2, 3}`.
* `(a - b) == {1}` :: Right. `a - b` keeps the items of `a` that are not in `b`, and that is only 1.
hint: Work out the three sets first: `a | b`, `a & b` and `a - b`.
hint: `a | b` is `{1, 2, 3, 4}`, `a & b` is `{2, 3}`, and `a - b` is `{1}`. Three of the five expressions are True.
right: Union holds everything once (`{1, 2, 3, 4}`), intersection holds what both share (`{2, 3}`), difference holds what only `a` has (`{1}`). And sets compare equal by their items, never by their order.
wrong: Write out each result first: `a | b` is `{1, 2, 3, 4}` (4 items, no duplicates), `a & b` is `{2, 3}`, and `a - b` is `{1}`. Then check each expression against those. Remember that `==` on sets ignores order.

=== task: code medium
title: Who hasn't arrived?
prompt: Write a function `missing_guests(invited, arrived)`. Both arguments are lists of names. Return a list, in alphabetical order, of the people who were invited but haven't arrived. Each name must appear only once, even if it's in `invited` twice.

For example, `missing_guests(["Ada", "Bo", "Cy"], ["Bo"])` returns `['Ada', 'Cy']`.
starter:
```python
def missing_guests(invited, arrived):
    missing = []
    # your code here
    return missing
```
solution:
```python
def missing_guests(invited, arrived):
    missing = set(invited) - set(arrived)
    return sorted(missing)
```
tests:
- missing_guests(["Ada", "Bo", "Cy"], ["Bo"])
- missing_guests(["Ada"], ["Ada"])
- missing_guests(["Cy", "Ada", "Bo"], [])
- missing_guests(["Ada", "Bo", "Ada"], ["Bo"])
- missing_guests(["Bo"], ["Bo", "Zed"])
fail[1]: Ada and Cy were invited but didn't arrive, so the answer is the **list** `['Ada', 'Cy']`. Take invited minus arrived, in that order, then return `sorted(...)` of the result: `sorted()` gives back a list.
fail[3]: Nobody has arrived, so all three are missing, and they must come back in alphabetical order: `['Ada', 'Bo', 'Cy']`. Return `sorted(...)` of your result.
fail[4]: Ada is in the invited list twice but must appear only once. Turning the list into a set drops the duplicate.
fail[5]: Zed arrived without being invited. He isn't missing, and he shouldn't appear in the answer. Use `set(invited) - set(arrived)`: only invited people can be missing.
error[TypeError]: `-` works on sets, not on lists: two lists give `TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for -: 'list' and 'list'`. Turn both lists into sets first with `set(invited)` and `set(arrived)`.
hint: "Invited but not arrived" is the difference of two sets.
hint: `set(invited) - set(arrived)` gives the missing people as a set, without duplicates.
hint: Return `sorted(...)` of that set: it gives a list in alphabetical order.
right: `set(invited) - set(arrived)` keeps the invited people who aren't in the arrived set, drops duplicates, and ignores anyone who arrived uninvited. `sorted()` then returns them as a list in alphabetical order.
wrong: Turn both lists into sets, subtract the arrived set from the invited set (in that order), and return `sorted()` of the result. That handles duplicates, people who arrived uninvited, and the alphabetical order in one go.

=== task: predict hard
title: A result is a new set
code:
```python
a = {5, 1, 3}
b = {3, 4, 5}
both = a & b
a.add(4)
print(sorted(a | b))
print(sorted(both))
print(sorted(a - b))
```
wrong[[1, 3, 4, 5]\n[3, 4, 5]\n[1]]: `both` was built on line 3, before 4 was added to `a`. An operator makes a new, separate set from the values at that moment, so `both` stays `{3, 5}`.
wrong[[1, 3, 4, 5]\n[3, 5]\n[1, 4]]: 4 is in `b`, so `a - b` takes it away too. Only 1 is in `a` without being in `b`.
wrong[[3, 5]\n[1, 3, 4, 5]\n[1]]: You've swapped union and intersection. `|` gives everything in either set; `&` gives only what's in both.
wrong[[1, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5]\n[3, 5]\n[1]]: A union is still a set, so 3 and 5 appear only once even though they are in both `a` and `b`.
hint: `both = a & b` builds a brand-new set from what `a` and `b` hold **at that moment**.
hint: After line 4, `a` is `{1, 3, 4, 5}`. Does that change `both`?
hint: For `a - b`, start with `a` and take away everything that is also in `b`.
right: `both` was built on line 3, when `a` was `{1, 3, 5}`, so it holds 3 and 5 and doesn't change later. After `a.add(4)`, the union is `[1, 3, 4, 5]`, and `a - b` leaves only `[1]`, because 3, 4 and 5 are all in `b`.
wrong: Take it line by line. Line 3 builds a new set `both` from 3 and 5, the items in both sets right then. Line 4 changes `a` to `{1, 3, 4, 5}`, but `both` is a separate set and stays `{3, 5}`. The union of `a` and `b` is `{1, 3, 4, 5}`. `a - b` removes 3, 4 and 5 from `a`, leaving 1. `sorted()` prints each one as a list.

=== task: parsons hard
title: Find the repeated words
prompt: Arrange the lines so that the program prints every word that appears more than once, in alphabetical order:

```output
['be', 'to']
```

Use one set for the words seen so far and another for the repeated words. Not every line is needed.
lines:
```python
words = ["to", "be", "or", "not", "to", "be"]
seen = set()
repeated = set()
for word in words:
    if word in seen:
        repeated.add(word)
    else:
        seen.add(word)
print(sorted(repeated))
```
distractors:
```python
seen = {}
repeated.append(word)
```
hint: Make the two empty sets before the loop. Remember which brackets make an empty set.
hint: For each word: if you've seen it before, it's a repeat; otherwise, remember it in `seen`.
hint: Sets use `.add()`, never `.append()`. The `print` goes after the loop, at the left edge.
right: Each word is checked against `seen`: the first time, it goes into `seen`; any later time, it goes into `repeated`. Because `repeated` is a set, a word seen three times would still be listed once. `sorted()` prints the result in alphabetical order.
wrong: Order: the data, two empty sets made with `set()`, the loop, then `if word in seen:` with `repeated.add(word)` under it and `else:` with `seen.add(word)` under it. Print after the loop. `seen = {}` would make a dictionary, which has no `.add()`, and sets have no `.append()`.

=== task: code hard
title: The first repeat
prompt: Write a function `first_repeat(words)` that returns the first word to show up for a **second time** as you read the list from left to right. If no word repeats, return `None`.

For example, `first_repeat(["x", "y", "z", "y", "x"])` returns `'y'`. Both x and y repeat, but reading from the left, the second y (position 3) comes before the second x (position 4).
starter:
```python
def first_repeat(words):
    seen = set()
    # your code here
    return None
```
solution:
```python
def first_repeat(words):
    seen = set()
    for word in words:
        if word in seen:
            return word
        seen.add(word)
    return None
```
tests:
- first_repeat(["a", "b", "a"])
- first_repeat(["x", "y", "z", "y", "x"])
- first_repeat(["solo"])
- first_repeat([])
- first_repeat(["b", "a", "a", "b"])
- first_repeat(["Hi", "hi", "HI"])
fail[2]: x is the first word that has a repeat somewhere, but y is the first word to **show up again**: its second copy (position 3) comes before x's (position 4). Walk through the list once, and return a word the moment you meet it for the second time.
fail[3]: A list with one word has no repeats, so the function must return `None`. Put `return None` after the loop, so it only runs when the loop finishes without finding a repeat.
fail[5]: Reading from the left: b, a, then a again. The second "a" (position 2) comes before the second "b" (position 3), so the answer is `'a'`.
fail[6]: Capital letters count: "Hi", "hi" and "HI" are three different strings, so nothing repeats and the answer is `None`. Don't change the case of the words.
error[AttributeError]: If the message says `'set' object has no attribute 'append'`, use `seen.add(word)`: sets have no `.append()`. If it says `'dict' object has no attribute 'add'`, you made `seen` with `{}`, which is an empty dictionary. Use `set()` instead.
hint: Loop over the words, and keep a set of the words you have already seen.
hint: For each word, check `if word in seen:` first. If it is, that word is the answer, so return it straight away. If not, add it to `seen`.
hint: Put `return None` after the loop: you only get there if no word was ever seen twice.
right: The set remembers every word read so far. The first time `word in seen` is True, that word is the first one to show up for a second time, so returning it right away gives the answer. If the loop ends without a repeat, the function returns `None`.
wrong: Use the seen-set pattern: `for word in words:`, then `if word in seen: return word`, otherwise `seen.add(word)`. After the loop, `return None`. Checking `in seen` **before** adding the word is what makes the second copy of a word stand out.
